WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.140
Water's polarity gives it three jobs in the cell.

2
00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:11.060
First, it sticks to itself through a network of hydrogen bonds, which is cohesion.

3
00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:17.080
Second, it dissolves charged and polar molecules, wrapping each ion in a hydration

4
00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:22.700
shell. Third, and most important for this chapter, it rejects non-polar molecules,

5
00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:26.060
and that rejection is what drives the hydrophobic effect.

6
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.820
Water is never just the solvent; it's an active player.

7
00:00:30.633 --> 00:00:34.973
The hydrophobic effect is the exception that runs this chapter; it's driven by

8
00:00:34.973 --> 00:00:36.553
entropy, not by attraction.

9
00:00:37.193 --> 00:00:40.293
The second law says a system trends toward disorder.

10
00:00:40.853 --> 00:00:45.413
When nonpolar molecules sit alone in water, the water has to freeze into ordered

11
00:00:45.413 --> 00:00:48.793
cages around them, which is low entropy and unfavorable.

12
00:00:49.653 --> 00:00:54.353
Cluster the nonpolar groups together and you release that caged water back into its

13
00:00:54.353 --> 00:00:55.993
free, high entropy state.

14
00:00:56.453 --> 00:01:00.913
The molecules huddle because water gains disorder by pushing them together, and that

15
00:01:00.913 --> 00:01:03.173
drives protein folding and membrane assembly.

16
00:01:03.967 --> 00:01:07.787
Here's a picture for the hydrophobic effect: a crowded dance floor.

17
00:01:08.187 --> 00:01:13.447
Scattered wallflowers, isolated non-polar molecules, force everyone else to

18
00:01:13.447 --> 00:01:18.167
navigate around them, and the surrounding water locks into low entropy cages.

19
00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:23.467
Push the wallflowers into one corner and the floor opens up, and the dancers, the

20
00:01:23.467 --> 00:01:24.867
water, are free to move.

21
00:01:25.287 --> 00:01:30.147
The force isn't attraction between the wallflowers. It's the system maximizing its

22
00:01:30.147 --> 00:01:31.967
freedom by getting them out of the way.

23
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.180
The same entropy that folds proteins builds your membranes.

24
00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.820
A phospholipid has two greasy tails that water won't wet.

25
00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.680
A single-tailed molecule is cone-shaped and packs into a sphere, a micelle.

26
00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:49.900
But a bulky two-tailed phospholipid is more cylindrical, so it can't form a tidy

27
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:55.280
sphere; instead it lines up tail to tail into a sheet, and that sheet closes into a

28
00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.060
bilayer that seals itself shut.

29
00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.160
No enzyme assembles it.

30
00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.020
Water does, by refusing to touch the tails.
